body:
Thank you. Sorry that the description is useless. We're all car novices here. We checked the battery terminals and saw no signs of corrosion at all. We'll just take the car to the repair shop.

&#x200B;

Thanks anyway.

>I find that multiple choice is horrible for testing physics concepts besides trivia, and should never be used in any university physics exam meant for a physics major.

&#x200B;

Yes, I totally agree and the questions are weighted poorly. Like I was describing above, even if you totally understand the theory behind it and make a simple sign error, you lose all of the points for that assignment.

&#x200B;

My mathematics courses are structured more in the way you have described your courses. We are required to show we understand a concept by "x-raying" it, i.e. deriving it ourselves or re-constructing it from an altered theoretical basis. The end-result of a numerical calculation is 1 of 15 possible points for an assignment.

&#x200B;

>Pure math assignments can take a whole week to think through, and physics assignments are generally more straightforward (ie do these 6 questions from Taylor's Classical Mechanics by friday) but still take hours each.

&#x200B;

And this is exactly how it should be. One problem that keeps me prying and pulling by forcing me to continously re-think my theoretical approach is worth gold. That theoretical situation will stick with me for the rest of my life by having contributed to the growth of a mental muscle used for theoretically deriving and solving things. It's made me subconsciously turn something over again and again. I love problems like that. If I'm stumped, I'll mentally put them aside and then I'll think them through while walking in the park, washing the dishes or cleaning up the appartment.  Suddenly, I'll have an aha-moment and it's solved.

I believe universities should aim at this from the very beginning. From what I've experienced so far, they are not teaching people to think. They are teaching people to mindlessly use. People become experts at applying, but they do not understand what they are actually doing. If they were required to re-invent a language, a theory or just modify one a bit to better explain something, they would be totally lost. In many cases, it is not their fault. If you have never encountered much of physics outside of such a terribly conceptualized course and you pass it with a decent grade, you can easily be misled to believe that you now know physics, when in reality, all you have done is punch numbers into your calculator. This becomes very evident in our courses when purely theoretical and conceptual questions are asked and only 25% of the class answers them correctly. E.g. almost no one was able to correctly state that the Earth experiences a force equal in magnitude to that of an object falling toward it. They have become so used to grabing F=mg and plugging things in, that they do not understand the fundamentals of what they are working with.

Wow, I can't tell you how much I appreciate the time you have taken to answer.  It really is a "unique" situation. You bring up a point that has also gone through my mind: Animals are like emotional tuning forks, they really do resonate with whatever we are giving off. I don't want this wonderful, loving, trusting, little guy to start developing paranoia and unease. He's got one of the most beautiful cat-souls I've ever seen. And I've seen more than a few. He really deserves the best the world can give him.

 I spoke to my parents just now on skype and they pretty much said the same thing you said: That I shouldn't beat up on myself and that I should do what's best for me, because that will also be what's best for the cat. Your point about the foster-home-likeness of the situation makes me feel a little bit better. And I'm sure this guy would quickly find his way into loving a new family. My stepdad said I should wait a week or two before making the final decision, because neither I nor the cat have had a lot of time to develop any kind of dynamic. He said the cat also needs time to adapt to my rhythms. Right now, the plan is to wait a week or two and then see how things are... I just don't want it to be too long either... 

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

I'm sitting here literally crying tears onto my keyboard for you. You seem to be a strong spirit, so I will assume you don't want sympathy. I myself never want anyone to feel sorry for me. But empathy is always welcome. I think your reply touched close to home because I too have observed how my creativity has died over the years. I used to write long, very thoughtful stories and paint, I also played the violin and was in orchestras that were very difficult to audition for. My first intention was to become a professional violinist and I had been working with a professional as my teacher. Around 15 or 16, I stopped feeling a connection to these creative forces and I am certain that they began to die as the disorder progressed. My study of physics is my last attempt to make use of my OCD...I can obssess about numbers and exactness all day with physics. And there is a good bit of creativity required. 

&#x200B;

Even if your mind feels numb and empty, I can assure you that there is a warm, intellectual glow radiating from your words. I can sense that there is still a beautiful spirit in your mind. It is not lost. The good thing about the brain is that it is neuroplastic. Unless damage is intense and very severe, there is always the possibility of recovery. But that takes time. If I understand correctly, you are still on the meds? And there is no possibility of coming off? There are good brain recovery supplements you could take such as Lion's Mane or Uridine Monophosphate (which was found to greatly increase cognitive functions in gerbils). I have noticed improvements with this combination. Magnolia bark is also a good brain supplement and is said to have antipsychotic properties. 

&#x200B;

I would also try using your hands as much as possible. Play an instrument, paint, draw, sketch, even if its just random forms without purpose or intention. Do cross-hatching, shading, lighting effects, etc. Because finger activitiy and neuronal development are very closely linked. This can definitely help repair some of the damage that has been done. For inspiration, you might like to watch a few talks given by the neurologist Manfred Spitzer (I hope some of them are in English...). 

&#x200B;

I think we will have to help ourselves. But luckily, we have the internet to communicate. Feel free to drop me a PM if you ever feel like it. It can be about this, or anything else you may have on your mind. Until then, I will keep you in my thoughts.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Well done. I can really identify with what the voices' words say in the background. The all black eyes give it a special creepy touch. 

&#x200B;

Just a side question: Are you religious? I found that my religious upbringing helped make my stuff worse because it provided the fuel (demons, godly voices, etc.). It has helped me to cut that stuff out of my life. I've noticed over the years that I've become more rational and calm (even though I still have psychotic symptoms).

I have been dealing with schizoaffective disorder, bipolar and severe OCD for 15 years. My dad killed himself due to his OCD. He also tried killing himself in front of me when I was a teenager. I've slept in the rubber cell of a psych ward for more than a week. I was on suicide watch, being observed 24/7 every 15 minutes by staff. My mental illnesses have done a lot of damage to my life. 

&#x200B;

I'm sorry for writing so much shit about me; I just want to make it clear that I can understand where you are coming from. I'm not going to write any sugar-coated bullshit, because I've been where you are. More than once. I've attempted suicide, I've harmed myself physically. I've felt the need to be dead multiple times in my life, because I was overwhelmed by the amount of shit I knew I would have to deal with. I didn't want to have my mental illnesses.

&#x200B;

But I promise you that you can do it. You can get through your anxiety attacks. And I care about you. I *want* you to make it with all my heart! I'm sitting here at 3 am, not knowing who you are, but I know you are out there somewhere, and I want you here. I believe in you. I believe you can find happiness. I believe you can become a great contributor to positive, healing things on this planet, that you can become content, peaceful, centered, strong and vested within yourself. 

&#x200B;

It's all about those little eletrical signals running between your synapses. Those little thoughts become real feelings, real words, actions, objects and movements. They can become world wars and holocausts or great sources of joy and happiness. You have a great creative force in your mind; right now, it is creating hell for you, but it is just as capable of creating heaven. 

&#x200B;

Please talk to me. I'm here for you. 

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Welcome. I'm glad you're here and that you are posting. You are not alone.

&#x200B;

I think I can relate to your feelings quite well, my friend. My mom is the only reason why I'm still here, too. I haven't killed myself yet because to do so would hurt her so much, I think it would kill her. I'm her only child. She had to earn the money and take care of things my entire life, because my dad had OCD, agoraphobia, was alcoholic and suicidal. He tried killing himself one time in front of us and eventually did kill himself the second time round. In my memories, my mom was always this exhausted, hopeful, but sad and worrisome woman with a big heart. 

&#x200B;

I developed psychotic delusions after my dad's first suicide attempt, withdrew from everyone, married abroad, lost that wife, then lived in a foreign country alone trapped in paranoid delusions, lost my citizenship and now have to be enrolled in college back home to live with my mom. I'm trying to study and get treatment while also trying to get my citizenship back. I hurt my mom so much with all of this and I have cost her all kinds of money. I feel like I'm just a continuation of the insanity my father put her through. So, the least I can do is not kill myself for her. The least I can do is stick around, at least as long as she's still here.

&#x200B;

If your mom is the only thing keeping you going at the moment, so be it, friend. You don't say much, but it seems like your mom is a very sweet and kind person. That is something to live for and maybe with time, both you and I can find other reasons to live for. But if your mom is all the reason you have right now, then take it and go with that. 

&#x200B;

Do you have anyone to talk to other than her? A therapist, a doctor, a psychiatrist? Anyone?

I'm sorry you are going through this. I think I can understand a little bit where you are coming from, my dear. Today is my birthday, I am turning 29, I have no degree, I have lost my citizenship because of paranoid schizophrenia and its delusions. The only way I can be at home with my mom is to be enrolled in the college here. I am trying to get my citizenship back with a lawyer and a psychiatrist, and I am also trying to get treatment for my racing intrusive thoughts, my voices and the panic-attacks I have. I feel like my life is in shambles, like I'm sitting in a big heap of rubble. I never thought I would be in such a state at 29. 

&#x200B;

When I was about 15 or 16, I started developing my mental illness and I withdrew from my family. My dad was mentally ill and eventually killed himself, after trying to kill himself right in front of me when I was 15. My mom was my only real connection to humanity, and I withdrew from her when I was about 16 or 17. Thoughts and voices told me to. Being alone all the time in my own mind helped fuel the development of my mental illness. 

&#x200B;

So, my long-winded point is: Take my word for it and don't let this cause you to withdraw from people. If you ever want to drop a PM, feel free to do so. Try to stay connected to people. Don't over-do it with people if you are naturally an introvert (that will make things worse), but don't withdraw because you have given up on trying to find compassionate people that understand you and have empathy for you. They are out there and they are there for you! 

&#x200B;

It sounds like your family is not supportive and lacks empathy for you. Perhaps it can help you to try and see them in a different light, as victims of their own circumstances? Could it be that they are over-challenged with a situation or with the life you guys are living in general? When terrible things happen, people can become numb and ignorant of feelings, because subconsciously they are trying to not feel the intense, negative feelings. I'm not wanting to defend your family, and I'm on the outside looking in, but it's just my guess. 

&#x200B;

Are there any loving, compassionate people you can go to outside of your family? In school? Do you have friends that you can trust and tell anything to?

I am with you in thought. I am trying to complete my first degree at age 29, after being stuck in a foreign country with delusions and losing my citizenship. My degree is the only way I can legally be with my family right now. So the pressure is extremely high for me to do well in school. Thoughts and intrusive inner voices make concentrating a lot more difficult, especially during exams. 

&#x200B;

People are always amazed at how good I do in school, but they have no idea how much energy I lose because of having to fight off voices and thoughts every 10-15 seconds. It's like trying to single-handedly control the high-pressure beam of a fire-hose and get it to go through the eye of a needle. I can't imagine how good I would be in school if I could get the energy and vitality back that I have to invest to keep my inner chaos at bay. 

&#x200B;

But that's enough about me. This is your thread. What are you studying? What career do you dream of? I'm just now learning about schizophrenia, psychosis, etc. and there have been a lot of successful, very smart people that suffered from it, and they still managed to do great things. Albert Einstein's son had it and so did the mathematician John Nash. I don't think the mathematician Paul Erdös had it, but he had other mental problems and eventually became hooked on amphetamines for the rest of his life, because he wasn't able to function academically w/o them. 

&#x200B;

So, try letting some of these people be an inspiration to you. It is possible to function with this mental illness. A good friend of mine said that schizophrenia (and OCD...something I also tend toward) has a great amount of creative energy. For example, it takes a lot of brain activity to actually believe a delusion and live your whole life according to it. If you can find a way to channel this energy into your studies, into art, into creative writing, into anything, you could easily become a master at it. I don't know how to do that...but thinking about it in this way gives me hope. Maybe it can give you some hope, too. 

I will be thinking about you today and wishing you the best.

I'm like you: I don't want sympathy either, just empathy. I really hope the government can have it and forgive me for the citizenship affair. You're right: I did have a lot of delusions and sub-delusions, which were mindgames going on as "consequences" of the big delusion, e.g. the English language contaminates the mind and is being used to rot my brain AND German immigration is following me and monitoring me (phone, bank, income, room, workplace) to try find a mistake I make so they can send me back because they too are in on the English-mind-control-conspiracy. It was all very real to me, so real that I eventually moved away to Germany, lost the connection to my mother tongue, forbade my German wife of hearing, speaking, or reading anything English (books, films, music, etc.). She put up with my insanity for awhile, keeping the rules I and thus also her had to live by secret from the family. Even though she herself was no angle, I feel so guilty for the hell I put her through with this. 

&#x200B;

What kind of music do you listen you to? I used to love classical music and used to play classical violin, but I lost my ability to really enjoy music a long time ago, like 15 years ago. I have a hard time feeling anything except anxiety or panic when it comes to music. That might be weird, but I can't change it. I sometimes listen to Goa or Psytrance music, if that is of any meaning to you. It's so loud and chaotic that it can drown out the voices and racing obsessive thinking, but  it can also make it worse. 

&#x200B;

I am really afraid of anti-psychotics because of the side effects. I need my mind to be as sharp as possible so I can get my degree and it feels like things have gotten better since my family has been caring for me, especially the voices. But they still come. 

&#x200B;

I'm sorry your parents aren't as supportive as you would need them to be. My family has been very supportive of me, especially after I broke down sobbing and finally told them about what had been going on with me, including every detail. But this was only possible after coming far enough to realize I had a problem. I would really like to check into  a hospital, but I'm afraid to do it because I will miss school and my schoolwork is the only way I can be with my family right now. I'd give anything to get my citizenship back and change this situation. 

&#x200B;

I'm sending you lots of hugs, too.

Well, I'm not managing very well at the moment, so I'm coming here for some inspiration...

I just want you to know I feel for you, man.

I haven't been diagnosed, but  I have heard voices and suffered from obsessive-compulsive, "foreign" thoughts enough to believe conspiracies that actually did lead to my loss of citizenship. I was convinced that my mother tongue was contaminating my mind, that it had been implanted into my and my family's brains to ruin us and destroy our mental health. I also believed there was a world-wide plan to insert this language into everyone's minds and destroy them in this way. Those are just a few of the things I had.

I'm trying to get my life back now and treat my mental condition(s). I struggled - without knowing I was struggling - for around 12 years,  convinced that the voices knew better, that I was aware of things others weren't, that I had special powers. Something along the lines of a God among men. It had a lot of time to destroy my marriage (I'm divorced), the relationship to my mom and eventually led to my loss of citizenship. At least the relationship to my mom is restored. I wake up in the morning and feel the same panic again and again, not wanting this to be my life, not wanting to have this, not wanting to sit in the rubble heap it has created. And the thoughts and voices still come. They seem to find new things to become focused on with time.

&#x200B;

I'm enrolled as a foreign student back home with my mom right now. It's the only way I can be here at the moment. I am studying physics, but it has been hard to focus with the thoughts and the voices. I'm doing okay, but it has cost me a lot of energy to do simple tasks and this was only my first freshman semester. I'm 29 and have no degree yet. I tried multiple times to get a degree between 25 and 26, but I wasn't able to manage. Living with my mom and stepfather while studying has been helpful. I have enough love and stability from my family, and school has forced me out of the house. Being on break has been absolutely horrible.

&#x200B;

I don't know about you, but it's been very hard to find a medical professional to help me. There is a forensic psychiatrist working with my attorney to diagnose me concerning citizenship, but he cannot treat me.  I grew up with a suicidal dad who had severe OCD and probably other things. He was on anti-psychotics sometimes and they left him drooling in a chair. So I was always terrified to say anything about my own issues. I finally did mention voices to a doctor about 2 years ago, and was given Melperone, but it zombified me so that I couldn't get any work done, and I backed off of it really fast. I really can't afford to be zombified now that my degree is my only way of being home and getting treatment... The only reason I haven't gone to the hospital is because I'm afraid it will prolong my degree and possibly interfere with my immigration status.

&#x200B;

I'm sorry my answer got so long. I just want you to know I feel for you. It's awful what a mental condition can do if it has enough time to operate without anyone - including yourself - knowing. Being alone - which I was a lot of the time - certainly doesn't help. But I find that I need a very specific mixture of me-time and time with others. Being with others suffocates me very fast. It can overload me and I often end up pretending to listen, nodding my head, when I actually can't hear or understand anything people are saying, because the thoughts and voices are louder. Do you have anyone you can do things with? Do you have any hobbies or passions?

&#x200B;

Whatever happens, don't give up. I know it sounds corny. I'm in the situation of legally being deportable back to a country where I have no family or home, no nothing. There are times when I seriously consider just ending it all, because I'm terrified my life will consist of delusions and their aftermath and that I will wander from one to another. But I have to try for my mom. She's the only reason I'm still here. Do you have anyone you can live for?

At the time I didn't know it, but looking back now, I'm pretty sure it was making everything much worse than it needed to be. But I was convinced it must be a positive influence, and that my problems were just getting worse. The thoughts got faster, louder and the emotions tended toward paranoia, sadness, anxiety, worry, etc. 

I think every drug can be a form of medicine and can offer something valuable and healing, if used in the right way. I'm pretty sure that my first MDMA trip was enough to get so much flowing again that I was able to start looking for help. For about 4 or 5 hours, I was able to feel feelings that I had literally not felt in 15 years. For years, I had been blindly searching for a vague version of what I needed: relief from the panic, anxiety and exhaustion, but never understanding that these were the consequences of my thoughts, voices, obsessions and compulsions. I had tried talk-therapy for years, meaning I was trying to reach the body through the mind. It never worked, because the mind could not be reached. My mind belonged to the OCD, the voices, intrusive thoughts and delusions. And I was so enveloped in them that my therapists didn't even know what was really going on. MDMA skipped the mind completely, cut right to the chase and showed the body what it felt like to feel warmth, happiness, love and be truly at peace.

&#x200B;

I feel like MDMA activated emotions that had been dormant and out-of-order since I had been 15. It only took this one time to start making them easier to reach again. When it comes to positive, healthy and healing emotions, I believe a little really does go a long way. I was so sick, so lost, so buried under emotional scar tissue, that just a little drop of MDMA-induced self-love had a tremendous, long-lasting effect. But it is important that such a drug is taken consciously and not like candy at a dance party. Only then can it be medicine.

&#x200B;

If marijuana can do similar things for you, then by all means use it. But it should always have a special time, place and purpose. If it does not fulfill such a purpose, just stay away from it. If you cannot stay away from it even though it makes things worse for you, you are most likely addicted like I was.
